WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.070
Film Courage: How does a writer protect themselves
from being taken advantage of?

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:08.130
Even though they have that…that they really
want that thing to pay off for them…

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.220
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:
Again, I think the most important thing a

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:15.230
writer can do is obviously they have to copyright
their material and that includes the treatments,

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:23.560
it includes the script, and you have to take
notes of where projects go.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.100
I look back at some of the projects I had
ripped off and if I had just taken better

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:33.590
notes, just documented - who, what, when,
where and why I could have stopped them from

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:38.370
being taken or at least or at least gotten
to partake in its success or failure from

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:47.410
a producer or a creator standpoint and I just
think it’s too easy to people when we’re

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.760
coming up we want so bad to have our work
accepted, we want to make it so bad whether

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.130
we’re an actor or a writer.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.590
The problem is if you’re an actor and you
want to make it, you can audition, you can

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:01.110
go out and meet people and do your thing but
your only calling card as a writer is to write

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.600
and unfortunately everything starts with the
script.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.450
You don’t have an actor attached, you don't
have a producer, a director, a set, financing

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:10.749
until - what’s the script?

00:01:10.749 --> 00:01:12.200
It’s all about the script.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.130
So you are kind of kicking off the whole project
and the most valuable thing that somebody

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.060
can take is a script, it’s the only thing
they can take.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.060
They can’t take an actor.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.530
They can’t take a director.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.930
They can’t steal a craft service person.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.590
They’re going to steal the script.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:31.219
You have to really think that you’re really
the lifeblood of the project, you are the

00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:37.960
inception of the project, and what you are
is gold to some people.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.609
So you have to protect it, you have to take
are much time as you’ve taken to write your

00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:43.609
script.

00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:44.609
You have to think I have to copyright it.

00:01:44.609 --> 00:01:48.429
I have to copyright every bit of intellectual
property that surrounds it whether it’s

00:01:48.429 --> 00:01:53.060
your beat sheets, your break downs, your boards,
whatever it is.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:59.499
You have to protect everything and then you
have to really, really be clear on who you

00:01:59.499 --> 00:02:02.590
have given it to.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.469
Hungry writers (I can’t tell you how often
I hear) well there is this producer reached

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:12.760
out to me on Facebook and I sent them three
of my scripts and I never heard from them

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:13.760
again.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.750
Well it’s like okay…did you look up who
the producer was?

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:22.640
Well you know he had this AC credit on a film
two years ago and if a real producer wants

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.870
to work with you they are not going to find
you on Facebook usually…now I understand

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:28.700
that is a way to find people.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.489
But how did they find you on Facebook?

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:35.350
Did they read something else you wrote and
Facebook was the only way to find you or were

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:42.469
they just trolling for you in the writing
forums, in the writing…what are they called…the

00:02:42.469 --> 00:02:46.019
writing forums on Facebook and that’s where
a lot of these people get taken advantage

00:02:46.019 --> 00:02:47.019
of.

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:48.019
It’s really easy.

00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:50.860
All you have to do is DM [direct message]
one writer who is writing something on the

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.650
board that you think is intelligent, gullible,
vulnerable and hungry enough and just say

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:57.980
hey hear is who I am.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.150
I’m interested in what you’ve got.

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:03.690
I’d love to read your work and nine times
out of nine they will probably going to send

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:04.690
it.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:05.690
Screen cap those conversations.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.739
Get this guy’s name, look him up.

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:09.680
Start a file on everybody you send stuff to.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.959
You should have a file on every project and
a file on everybody who sees whatever project

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:17.420
you have and that’s a big way to protect
yourself.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.060
Film Courage: A dossier.

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.349
Shane: Is that what that is?

00:03:20.349 --> 00:03:26.620
I’ve heard new words like there’s the
dossier and what’s the other one - an Op-Ed

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:27.620
[commonly known as "opposite the editorial
page” in journalism].

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.379
I’ve heard these new words the last four
years that I never knew before.

00:03:31.379 --> 00:03:35.450
Film Courage: And in the reverse, how can
someone who has been asked to read their script

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.359
vet a producer?

00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:44.920
So seeing just like one credit on IMDB with
no picture, no contact info, very little online

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.080
presence.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.129
Is that someone who you should really be sending
your work to?

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.970
Shane: I always say that it’s important
for people to vet on the other side.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:04:00.079
We’ve talked about before, the easiest thing
to do if you’re a person who has a business

00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:03.609
card who says you’re a producer but you
haven’t physically produced.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:07.629
The easiest thing to do is to go to something
like Facebook and find a writer’s forum

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:10.930
and find people that are…you know all you
have to do is join, it’s free to join and

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:15.030
you get to see the trends and habits and some
of the idiosyncrasies of the people that are

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:19.519
on that site and you start realizing their
vulnerabilities, their hopes, their fears,

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:22.750
their concerns and where they are careless.

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:28.550
I find it amazing that people will actually
say here is my latest script and throw it

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.889
on a Facebook forum and you go oh my gosh.

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:35.110
Or they will go hey I just did these 10 pages
last night.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.740
Would love feedback, okay.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:47.210
I think that’s careless but producers are
predatory by nature and sorry but they are.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:51.530
You have something that they want so I think
that you need to protect your work.

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:55.930
I think you need to think twice before you
just shotgun your material out.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:57.270
I think you need to look up people.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:01.229
There's a lot of writers that I respect that
I know and I still try to collaborate with

00:05:01.229 --> 00:05:05.580
that will say Oh I heard from a producer,
I sent this guy my work, he reached out to

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:09.080
me and I’m looking at it and I’m going
have you even…oh no I know he doesn’t

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:12.919
have an IMDB presence or he doesn’t even
have but he’s you know and I’m like not

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:19.080
that IMDB is the end all be all but goodness
if someone has got a history in the business

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.500
of being legitimate that’s certainly one
way to vet them.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:30.080
It’s mind-boggling how often people don’t
take the time, they don’t want to hear the

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.701
truth so they just shotgun it hoping that
this could be the one and I think that they

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:39.470
have to be very careful and hence getting
your projects ripped off several times over

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:45.990
your career because you’re not careful and
I gave my projects to people that were real

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:50.349
and legit but I didn't protect myself and
they know when you don’t.

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:56.000
Film Courage: So even if someone is vetted
it doesn’t necessarily mean that you’re

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.000
safe?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.470
Shane: Just because someone is vetted doesn’t
mean that you’re safe.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:07.610
The easiest thing to do is to get a script
that is not copy written and then have the

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:13.729
power to make something and get it from somebody
who has no clout or juice or voice or word

00:06:13.729 --> 00:06:20.540
in the industry if that’s the case then
what do they have to stand on if they’re

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:21.540
not protected?

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.930
Film Courage: What if someone says well then
maybe I just shouldn’t trust anybody?

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:25.930
Shane: You shouldn’t.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:27.409
Film Courage: Then why even bother doing this?

00:06:27.409 --> 00:06:30.430
Shane: Well you shouldn’t…don’t trust
anybody.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:34.650
What you should do is protect your work as
we talked about before with the United States

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:35.650
Copyright office.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:37.780
Don’t waste your time with WA, do it with
USO.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:43.780
It’s the same process…kind of…it costs
a little bit more but it shows people that

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:49.080
are in the know that you’re serious, it
shows that you know how to protect your work

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:54.430
and take copious notes and you should have
a file on each project and you should have

00:06:54.430 --> 00:06:59.800
a file on everybody that you send it to and
that should be part of it and those correspondences

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:04.270
and if it’s a phone call take notes “I
talked to Shane Stanley today, it’s October

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.069
1st, 2020.

00:07:06.069 --> 00:07:08.539
He said he wanted me to send a synopsis.

00:07:08.539 --> 00:07:12.889
Emailed Shane Stanley at this email address,
the synopsis for this story, waiting for a

00:07:12.889 --> 00:07:14.210
call back or an email.”

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:18.110
And then when I email or phone back, print
it out, put it in the file.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:23.510
And that way if I go off and make that movie
now the burden of proof is on me that I didn’t

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:24.510
rip them off.

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:28.780
It’s always on the writer to prove that
I ripped you off but now all of the sudden

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:33.520
if you do your due diligence it's now on me
to show that I have to now provide that I

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.232
did to develop the material and that's harder
to do.

00:07:36.232 --> 00:07:40.490
Film Courage: Do you do this in Excel or a
Word or Pages document?

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:45.639
Shane: What I usually do is I have a back
up email address like if I have my company

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:50.520
account whatever-it-is-dot-com I have a Yahoo
account that I don’t access or give out

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:52.910
to anybody, all it is is my overages.

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:58.599
It’s every time I email a project to somebody
I bcc it [blind carbon copy] and then that

00:07:58.599 --> 00:08:05.030
way I have it safe and then when that person
replies I have a folder on my main of each

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:06.030
project.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:10.750
So every time I get a hit I’m getting…I
got the send out sent somewhere else and then

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:14.590
I’ve got the reply in a whole different
other email mass so that way gosh forbid one

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:19.729
crashes, one goes blank or gets hacked I at
least have half the conversation saved somewhere.

00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:21.520
That’s how I do it.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:22.520
Film Courage: That’s great.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:23.520
Question for the viewers: What do you think
are the best ways for a screenwriter to protect

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:23.522
their work?

